			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-success-11)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-success-12)" />
		</div>
		<h3 id="scale-warning"><code>--ui-color-warning-*</code></h3>
		<div
			class="docs-scale"
			role="img"
			aria-label="Warning scale, from step 1 to 12"
		>
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-1)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-2)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-3)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-4)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-5)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-6)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-7)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-8)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-9)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-10)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-11)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-warning-12)" />
		</div>
		<h3 id="scale-danger"><code>--ui-color-danger-*</code></h3>
		<div
			class="docs-scale"
			role="img"
			aria-label="Danger scale, from step 1 to 12"
		>
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-1)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-2)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-3)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-4)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-5)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-6)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-7)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-8)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-9)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-10)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-11)" />
			<span style="background: var(--ui-color-danger-12)" />
		</div>
		<h2 id="tokens">Usage tokens</h2>
		<p>
			Read the usage token, not the step:
			<code>--ui-color-border</code>, not <code>--ui-color-surface-6</code>. No loose hex, size or font: if you miss something, it is added to UI. The whole contract is in <code>packages/ui/DESIGN.md</code>.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="app/styles/main.css"
			bind:code="examples.themeUse"
		/>
		<DocsApi
			caption="Main tokens"
			bind:rows="[
				{ name: '--ui-color-bg / -bg-subtle', type: 'color', description: 'Background of the page and quiet bands.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-surface / -surface-raised', type: 'color', description: 'Card, panel and table; popover and modal.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-control / -hover / -active', type: 'color', description: 'Background of a control and its states.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-text / -text-soft', type: 'color', description: 'Text and secondary text.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-border / -border-strong / -control-border', type: 'color', description: 'Separators, quiet borders and the border of a field at 3:1 or more.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-primary / -hover / -contrast', type: 'color', description: 'Main action and the text on it.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-primary-soft / -text / -border', type: 'color', description: 'A quiet active background, with its text and its border. Badge, Alert, Toast and Avatar paint with the steps -3, -11 and -7 of the scale.' },
				{ name: '--ui-color-danger / -soft / -border / -text', type: 'color', description: 'Info, success and warning have the same shape.' },
				{ name: '--ui-font-size-xs … -6xl', type: 'rem', description: 'Text sizes: reading at lg, controls at md, titles from 2xl up.' },
				{ name: '--ui-space-* / --ui-radius-*', type: 'rem', description: 'Space in steps of 0.25rem and radii from xs to xl.' },
				{ name: '--ui-control-height-sm / -md / -lg', type: 'rem', description: 'Heights of the controls, which grow by themselves with a touch pointer.' },
				{ name: '--ui-duration-* / --ui-ease-*', type: 'time', description: 'Motion: fast for color, base and slow for a panel.' },
			]"
		/>
		<h2 id="scale">Fluid scale</h2>
		<p>
			The interface grows with the screen in one place, the global base: 16 px up to 1440, 18 px at 2560 and 21 px at 3840, always from the font size of the browser. Every token is in
			<code>rem</code>, so they grow together. What is written in
			<code>px</code>
			does not grow.
			<code>{'ui: { base: false }'}</code>
			removes the base, and the scale with it.
		</p>
		<h2 id="brand">Your brand</h2>
		<p>
			The tokens of UI come with
			<code>@import '@meekojs/ui'</code>
			in your
			<code>app/styles/main.css</code>, and the rules you write after the imports win: override the steps of a scale and everything that reads them changes with them.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="app/styles/main.css"
			bind:code="examples.themeCss"
		/>
		<h3 id="semantic-color">Change a semantic color</h3>
		<p>
			Each semantic color (<code>primary</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>neutral</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>warning</code> and <code>danger</code>) reads the twelve steps of its scale,
			<code>--ui-color-primary-1</code>
			to
			<code>-12</code>, and its contrast text, <code>--ui-color-primary-contrast</code>. To make it read another scale, point those steps at it. The optional entry
			<code>@meekojs/ui/hues</code>
			brings the scales of Radix Colors, light and dark, as
			<code>--color-crimson-1</code>
			to
			<code>-12</code>
			and
			<code>--color-crimson-contrast</code>. The
			<code>:root[data-theme]</code>
			selector makes the rule also win over the steps of UI's dark theme; the Radix scales already follow the theme.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="app/styles/main.css"
			bind:code="examples.themeColor"
		/>
		<p>
			If your site already has its variables,
			<code>ui.tokens</code>
			accepts CSS values or references to them, and each key assigns its
			<code>--ui-*</code>
			tokens.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="meeko.config.ts"
			bind:code="examples.theme"
		/>
		<DocsApi
			caption="Keys of ui.tokens"
			bind:rows="[
				{ name: 'primary / primaryHover / onPrimary', type: 'string', description: '--ui-color-primary and step 9 of its scale, its hover and step 10, and the text on it.' },
				{ name: 'accentSoft / accentText', type: 'string', description: 'Backgrounds and text of active states: --ui-color-primary-soft and -text, and steps 3 and 11.' },
				{ name: 'surface / surfaceHover', type: 'string', description: 'Surfaces and controls, and their states.' },
				{ name: 'text / textSoft / muted', type: 'string', description: 'Main and secondary text.' },
				{ name: 'border / borderStrong', type: 'string', description: 'Separators and outlines.' },
				{ name: 'focus / danger', type: 'string', description: 'Keyboard focus and errors: danger also sets steps 9 and 11 of its scale.' },
				{ name: 'font / radius', type: 'string', description: 'Font family and base radius.' },
				{ name: 'controlHeight / controlPadding', type: 'string', description: 'Dimensions of the actions.' },
				{ name: 'controlWeight / controlFontSize', type: 'string', description: 'Weight and text size of the actions.' },
			]"
		/>
		<h2 id="classes">Classes of each component</h2>
		<p>
			The components are painted with Tailwind 4 classes, without a prefix, which your application compiles together with its own in
			<code>app/styles/main.css</code>. Those classes read the tokens, so everything above also changes the components. To adjust a particular one,
			<code>class</code>
			reaches its root element and the
			<code>ui</code>
			prop adds classes to each part, by the name of its slot;
			<code>class</code>
			is the same as
			<code>ui.base</code>. The slot names are in the props type of each component, and the editor completes them. <code>UIButton</code>, <code>UILink</code>, <code>UIIconButton</code>, <code>UICopyButton</code>,
			<code>UICard</code>
			and
			<code>UILinkCard</code>
			merge
			<code>class</code>
			with Tailwind Merge: your utility replaces the recipe's one that sets the same property.
		</p>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.themeSlots" />
		<p>
			In the rest, your classes are added to those of the component, without removing any. The styles of UI follow Tailwind's layers: the global base goes in
			<code>ui-base</code>, right above the preflight; what is written by hand, in <code>components</code>, and the utilities, in <code>utilities</code>, next to yours. Any unlayered rule of your application wins over all of them, whatever its specificity. So does a rule on an element, such as <code>{'a { color: … }'}</code>: scope it to your content if you do not want to change the UI links.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="app/styles/main.css"
			bind:code="examples.themeSlotsCss"
		/>
		<h3 id="migration">From the meeko-ui-* classes</h3>
		<p>
			The
			<code>.meeko-ui-*</code>
			classes of the components and the
			<code>data-*</code>
			attributes that only served for styling no longer exist: a rule that selected them stops applying without any error. Give the component a name of your own with
			<code>class</code>
			or
			<code>ui</code>
			and change the selector to that name. If the rule depended on a variant, such as
			<code>[data-variant='outline']</code>, put the class where you use that variant. The attributes that JavaScript reads (<code>data-meeko-*</code>) and ARIA are still there.
		</p>
		<DocsApi
			caption="Most common equivalents"
			bind:rows="[
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-button[data-variant]', type: 'class + variant', description: 'UIButton and UILink: the variant and color props (and size, radius) and your class.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-badge / -badge-dot', type: 'ui.base / ui.dot', description: 'UIBadge.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-input / -textarea', type: 'class', description: 'UIInput and UITextarea.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-field-label / -error / -description', type: 'ui.label / ui.error / ui.description', description: 'UIField; also ui.meta and ui.counter.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-choice / -choice-content', type: 'ui.base / ui.content', description: 'UICheckbox and UISwitch; the control is ui.input.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-card-header / -title / -body / -footer', type: 'ui.header / ui.title / ui.body / ui.footer', description: 'UICard; class is ui.base.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-table-scroll / .meeko-ui-table', type: 'ui.base / class', description: 'UITable; also ui.cell, ui.headerCell, ui.bodyRow. Replaces --table-border, --table-background and --table-hover.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-modal-heading / -body / -footer', type: 'ui.heading / ui.body / ui.footer', description: 'UIModal; class is ui.base, the <dialog>.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-alert-title / -description', type: 'ui.title / ui.description', description: 'UIAlert; the variant and color props replace data-tone.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-page-main / -sidebar / -aside', type: 'ui.main / ui.sidebar / ui.aside', description: 'UIPage.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-header-nav / -actions', type: 'ui.nav / ui.actions', description: 'UIHeader.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-command-row / -excerpt', type: 'ui.row / <small>', description: 'UICommandPalette; the active row has aria-selected.' },
				{ name: '.meeko-ui-tooltip / -tooltip-trigger', type: 'class / triggerClass', description: 'UITooltip and UIPopover.' },
			]"
		/>
		<p>
			<code>MeekoSelect</code>,
			<code>MeekoPopover</code>
			and
			<code>MeekoThemeToggle</code>, deprecated in the core, no longer get their look from UI: use <code>UISelect</code>,
			<code>UIPopover</code>
			and
			<MeekoLink to="/theme-toggle">UIThemeToggle</MeekoLink>. The
			<code>meeko-ui-prose</code>
			class, which dresses Markdown on any element, and the
			<code>meeko-ui-sr-only</code>
			utility stay public API, since they do not depend on Tailwind's classes.
		</p>
		<h2 id="defaults">Default values</h2>
		<p>
			<code>ui.defaults</code>
			gives every instance of a component the same classes. The key is its name without the prefix, in camelCase, such as
			<code>card</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>descriptionList</code>…; every component that accepts
			<code>class</code>
			or
			<code>ui</code>
			accepts both here. They add up to those of each instance: first those of the component, then those of
			<code>ui.defaults</code>
			and last those of the instance. Without class merging, the cascade decides a conflict: your unlayered CSS wins.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="meeko.config.ts"
			bind:code="examples.themeDefaults"
		/>
		<p>
			A few variants also accept a base value:
			<code>variant</code>, <code>color</code>,
			<code>size</code>
			and
			<code>radius</code>
			in
			<code>button</code>, <code>link</code>,
			<code>iconButton</code>
			and
			<code>copyButton</code>; <code>variant</code>, <code>color</code>, <code>radius</code>, <code>elevation</code>,
			<code>padding</code>
			and
			<code>density</code>
			in
			<code>card</code>, and
			<code>density</code>
			in
			<code>table</code>. The explicit prop of the instance always wins. Defaults are validated when the module loads and isolated per application and SSR request.
		</p>
		<h2 id="messages">Texts and languages</h2>
		<p>
			The few texts components paint on their own (the close button of
			<code>UIModal</code>, the empty state of
			<code>UITable</code>
			and
			<code>UICombobox</code>, the names of <code>UIThemeToggle</code>) come in Spanish and English. With
			<code>@meekojs/i18n</code>
			they follow the language of the document, without any configuration. Without that module,
			<code>ui.locale</code>
			chooses the language; Spanish by default.
			<code>ui.messages</code>
			overrides texts or adds other languages.
		</p>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.messages" />
		<p>
			A prop or a snippet of the component always win over these texts. What a language lacks comes from its base language (<code>pt</code>
			for
			<code>pt-BR</code>), from
			<code>ui.locale</code>
			and, last, from Spanish. The server and the client choose the same text, so hydration changes nothing.
		</p>
		<p>
			The types
			<code>UIOptions</code>,
			<code>UITokens</code>
			and
			<code>UIDefaults</code>
			are exported from
			<code>@meekojs/ui</code>.
		</p>
	</DocsPage>
</template>
